            <ArticleTitle>Comparison of mental health treatment status and use of antidepressants in men and women with eating disorders.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>1-15</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.1080/10640266.2017.1383788</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>Mental health treatment status and antidepressant use were investigated among men and women with an eating disorder (ED) who were interviewed in a general population survey of 3005 adults (aged ≥15 years). Compared to women, men with an ED were significantly less likely to receive treatment for a mental health problem or to be currently using an antidepressant. On multivariate analyses, female gender, lower mental health-related quality of life, and lower weight/shape overvaluation were significant predictors of receiving treatment and antidepressant use. Treatment was less likely in men and in people with higher ED cognitions.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
